SnapRAID Daemon TODO
====================

2.x Releases
============

- Add a new "notify_start" that can be used to notify the start of a high level task like maintenance.
  - Add a macro for the subject %s like notify_result.
  - Add a macro in notify_result and notify_start for the task name. Like %t/maintenance/heal/undelete...
  - Add a macro in notify_result for the exit code %c.

- For every change (pulse) detected while running commands, emit a message
  shown in the task messages. Like:
  Disk d2 changed path from xxx to yyyy
  Disk d3 changed UUID from xxx to yyyy
  Device /dev/sda changed serial from xxx to yyyy
  Device /dev/sda changed power from standby to active
  Device /dev/sda changed smart attribute XXXX from xxx to yyy

- Send a notification report every time a PREFAIL attribute changes.
  Messages can be generated in the parser, and then a dedicated report can be
  scheduled at the end of each task.
  However, a system is needed to ignore some of them.
  If done via CLI, they will no longer be visible. It would be better to do it from the daemon.
  New configurations option are required.
    notify_smartignore = DISK/DEVICE/* ATTRS...
    notify_smartignore = parity 188 command_timeout protocol_error (disables the notification, but not the display)
    smartignore = * 188 command_timeout protocol_error (completly disables the attribute everywhere, similar to disabling it in the CLI)

- Count data data/io error happened in the past (monotonic increasing, never resetting to 0).
  Show this counter in yellow.
  > past_error_data
  > past_error_io

- Allow to disable scheduled sync but keep scrub. A check flag to enable/disable.

- In manual maintenance allow to enable/disable sync and select how much to scrub.
  Use as default the settings.

- Allow to customize the DAEMON/SERVICE_NAME tags to allow multiple
  daemon/services at the same time.
  It can be automatically imported from the executable name.
  Unify DAEMON/SERVICE_NAME in DAEMON_TAG.
  Add also support to customize the snapraid command line.
  Required to use a different snapraid.conf for selecting the array.

- Unprivileged execution mode
  - Starts as 'root' (Unix) or 'SYSTEM' (Windows) to parse config and open raw
    disk descriptors.
  - Forks immediately: Parent stays 'root' (Monitor), Child drops permanently to 
    unprivileged user like 'nobody' (Worker) via setuid().
  - Worker runs the web server, handles network IO, and processes data.
  - Processes communicate over a private socketpair() using strict binary opcodes.
  - To perform privileged actions, Worker sends an integer opcode. Monitor
    validates it against a whitelist and executes it natively.
  - Worker cannot pass arbitrary paths, strings, or shell commands to Monitor.
  - Monitor opens logs/state files as root and passes the file descriptors to the
    Worker via SCM_RIGHTS.
  - Worker writes directly to descriptors, avoiding IPC logging overhead and
    eliminating write permissions to /var/log/.
  - Systemd & BSD Init: Starts simple as root; application handles its own
    sandboxing without needing sudo or complex user provisioning scripts.
  - Windows: Maps natively by spawning a restricted worker token via 
    CreateProcessAsUser().
   
Future Releases
===============

- Support hw-probe to send data to linuxhw.
  https://github.com/linuxhw/hw-probe#appimage
  - Download appimage, check it's hash, and run it with -all -upload
  - Otherwise find a simpler way to just send the smartctl output

- Import historical SMART data from CSV files generated by smartd 
  (stored at `/var/lib/smartmontools/attrlog.MODEL-SERIAL.ata.csv`).

- Short and long SMART self-test results should be displayed.

- Add Apprise notification support. It support more than 100 services.
  https://appriseit.com/

- Add benchmarks with FIO. https://github.com/axboe/fio
  This process runs a random read and write of two 128MB files created
  during execution of fio.                                               │  
  $ fio --name=global --rw=randread --size=128m --name=job1 --name=job2

- Implement Windows installer signature with SignPath.io

- Implement a Command Builder panel to support manual triggers for on-demand
  operations (sync/scrub/etc.), advanced arguments (bandwidth limits, stats,
  filtering), and custom job sequencing.

- Speedometer
  During an active task, show the same speed stats line "snapraid -A"

- Allow to accept a SMART attribute value as valid/acknowledged and don't show
  it anymore in the disk card UI as warning. It will remain in the full SMART 
  attributes list.

- Stress Test Utility
  Provide a way to stress-test new disks before adding them to the array.
  A "Badblocks" or "Long SMART Test" trigger from the UI.
  UI Integration: A dedicated "Maintenance" tab for non-array disks 
  to ensure they are healthy before they become critical to data integrity.

- SMART Trend Analysis
  Extend the SMART attribute history to allow to draw a graph of the attribute trend
  Instead of just a notification, the daemon reports full history of the attrinute.
  The Web UI could then render a trend graph. 

- Spin-down Analytics.
  Track how many hours each disk spends in "Standby" vs "Active" over a week.
  UI Integration: A "Power Savings" widget on the Dashboard showing the percentage
  of time the array was successfully spun down

- Searchable Task Archives
  In the Tasks page, add a search bar that filters historical logs for specific filenames or error codes.

- Implement initial configuration of SnapRAID directly through the Web UI

- Disk Replacement Wizard
  Replacing a failed disk is the most stressful time for a SnapRAID user.
  - Step-by-Step Recovery UI.
  - Select/detect the failed disk.
  - Select the new empty disk.
  - The UI generates the exact snapraid fix -d [diskname] command and monitors
    progress with a high-priority "Recovery" progress bar on the Dashboard.

- macOS version of the daemon:
  - Extend the SnapRAID CLI to build the list of low-level devices from
    a filesystem. This will enable smartctl support on macOS.
  - Implementation starting point: devtree(), which retrieves the list of
    low-level devices associated with a filesystem device for use
    with smartctl.

Something like this:

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <sys/param.h>
#include <sys/mount.h>
#include <CoreFoundation/CoreFoundation.h>
#include <IOKit/IOKitLib.h>
#include <IOKit/storage/IOMedia.h>

typedef struct {
    char **disks;
    int count;
    int capacity;
} DiskList;

// Initialization with capacity tracking for efficiency
void init_disk_list(DiskList *list) {
    list->count = 0;
    list->capacity = 4; // Start with small buffer
    list->disks = malloc(sizeof(char *) * list->capacity);
}

void free_disk_list(DiskList *list) {
    if (!list->disks) return;
    for (int i = 0; i < list->count; i++) {
        free(list->disks[i]);
    }
    free(list->disks);
    list->disks = NULL;
    list->count = 0;
}

// Robustly adds a disk to the list, preventing duplicates and handling realloc failures
int add_to_list(DiskList *list, const char *bsd_name) {
    if (!list || !bsd_name) return -1;

    // Check for duplicates
    for (int i = 0; i < list->count; i++) {
        if (strcmp(list->disks[i], bsd_name) == 0) return 0;
    }

    // Grow buffer if needed
    if (list->count >= list->capacity) {
        int new_capacity = list->capacity * 2;
        char **new_disks = realloc(list->disks, sizeof(char *) * new_capacity);
        if (!new_disks) return -1; // Memory error
        list->disks = new_disks;
        list->capacity = new_capacity;
    }

    list->disks[list->count] = strdup(bsd_name);
    if (!list->disks[list->count]) return -1;
    list->count++;
    return 0;
}

// The recursive crawler with safety checks
void find_physical_disks(io_registry_entry_t entry, DiskList *list) {
    io_iterator_t iter;
    kern_return_t kr = IORegistryEntryGetParentIterator(entry, kIOServicePlane, &iter);
    
    if (kr != KERN_SUCCESS) return;
    if (!iter) return;

    io_registry_entry_t parent;
    while ((parent = IOIteratorNext(iter))) {
        // Look for IOMedia objects
        if (IOObjectConformsTo(parent, kIOMediaClass)) {
            CFBooleanRef is_whole = IORegistryEntryCreateCFProperty(parent, CFSTR(kIOMediaWholeKey), kCFAllocatorDefault, 0);
            
            if (is_whole) {
                if (CFBooleanGetValue(is_whole)) {
                    // This is a 'Whole' physical disk (e.g., disk0)
                    CFStringRef bsd_name = IORegistryEntryCreateCFProperty(parent, CFSTR(kIOBSDNameKey), kCFAllocatorDefault, 0);
                    if (bsd_name) {
                        char buffer[MAXPATHLEN];
                        if (CFStringGetCString(bsd_name, buffer, MAXPATHLEN, kCFStringEncodingUTF8)) {
                            char full_path[MAXPATHLEN];
                            snprintf(full_path, MAXPATHLEN, "/dev/%s", buffer);
                            add_to_list(list, full_path);
                        }
                        CFRelease(bsd_name);
                    }
                }
                CFRelease(is_whole);
            }
        }

        // Recursively climb. This handles RAID (multiple parents) and APFS
        find_physical_disks(parent, list);
        IOObjectRelease(parent);
    }
    IOObjectRelease(iter);
}

DiskList get_physical_disks_for_path(const char *path) {
    DiskList list;
    init_disk_list(&list);

    if (!path) return list;

    struct statfs st;
    if (statfs(path, &st) != 0) {
        // Possible reasons: Path doesn't exist, Permission denied
        return list; 
    }

    const char *bsd_name = st.f_mntfromname;
    // Strip /dev/ if present for IOKit matching
    if (strncmp(bsd_name, "/dev/", 5) == 0) bsd_name += 5;

    CFMutableDictionaryRef matching = IOBSDNameMatching(kIOMasterPortDefault, 0, bsd_name);
    if (!matching) return list;

    io_service_t service = IOServiceGetMatchingService(kIOMasterPortDefault, matching);
    if (service) {
        // Case 1: The mount itself is a whole disk (unlikely for filesystems, but possible)
        CFBooleanRef is_whole = IORegistryEntryCreateCFProperty(service, CFSTR(kIOMediaWholeKey), kCFAllocatorDefault, 0);
        if (is_whole && CFBooleanGetValue(is_whole)) {
            char full_path[MAXPATHLEN];
            snprintf(full_path, MAXPATHLEN, "/dev/%s", bsd_name);
            add_to_list(&list, full_path);
        } else {
            // Case 2: Standard case - climb the tree to find physical ancestors
            find_physical_disks(service, &list);
        }
        
        if (is_whole) CFRelease(is_whole);
        IOObjectRelease(service);
    }

    return list;
}
